{"title":"Emergent Behavior & Causal Relationships in exp-E-decision","narrative":"This evacuation simulation involved 100 agents moving at 1.333 m/s through a single 3-cell (1.2 m) east exit on a 38×38 grid. Collisions are blocked, and agents stop when they exit. The run finished within the configured 300 steps, but no per-step evacuation count or density profiles are provided in the evidence.

Without time-series data on evacuation times, door throughput, or local densities, we cannot rigorously identify system-level patterns such as clogging, lane formation, or oscillatory flows. The static specification (door width vs. agent count and speed) suggests potential for transient arch‐like congestions at the exit, but no quantitative evidence was recorded to validate or quantify such phenomena.

Because only the model configuration and run completion status are available—without aggregated occupancy, flow rates, or shape property series—no causal chains between agent decisions or LLM calls and macroscopic evacuation metrics can be extracted. Future runs should log per-step evacuated counts or density heatmaps to enable identification of emergent behaviors and causal effects of decision-making policies.","key_findings":["100 agents evacuate through a 1.2 m exit at 1.333 m/s on a 38×38 grid.","Run completed under 300 steps, but no time-series data on exit flows was recorded.","Collision blocking ensures no overlap, likely inducing transient jams at the door.","No shape property series or density metrics are available to detect environment feedback loops.","Lack of evacuation time series precludes quantification of emergent patterns or policy effects."],"causal_links":[],"emergent_phenomena":[]}
